as maybe half mile so  yeah you know rough you know 10 minutes  or so so yeah that was fine  that didn't bother me unfortunately i  think the vast majority of commuters  have considerably more  time so so i think that's a great thing  and i think it's it's good of course  from environmental perspective uh  you know where people don't have  obviously a lot of resources are wasted  in uh  commuting so so that's a great story um  four day work week i think similar story  again if you compare the us to europe  um there have been sharp reductions in  work hours over the last four decades in  i was going to say most all european  countries and  we now are average work year in the  united states 20 25  longer than it is in germany and  netherlands denmark you know other  wealthy countries  and i think most people do value their  time and if we could have a four day  work week rather than a five that's  great and yeah i'd love to see the hours  cut because i think a 10 hour day  certainly in a lot of jobs people would  find exhausting so  if we can make it a nine hour day uh  maybe somewhere down the road make an  eight hour day but whatever  uh nine hours isn't that much worse than  eight so you know i think  most people consider that pretty good  deal and again getting back to the  commuting if you have four commutes  rather than five that's  that's a plus so i'd love to see us get  to  shorter work years and a four-day work  week's really good way to go  uh without a doubt i'm i'm with you  there dean uh we've been speaking with  dean  baker who's a senior economist at the  center for economic and policy research  also a visiting professor at the  university of utah  always great having you on well thanks  for having me on good questions
 

